# Source location: https://github.com/MelkhiorVintageComputing/RD68041
#
# The MC68LC040 guest's command set (M13; doc/virt.md): commands.script without
# the floating point, which on this part is the kernel's FPU emulation and has no
# transcendental functions -- awk's sin, exp and log print what the emulation
# makes of them (RD68031's caution, doc/oracles.md) -- and with a line of the
# arithmetic the emulation does have instead. Its own note follows.
#
# The scripted command set of `make virt-qemu` (sim/virt/linux/qemu-run.py):
# `expect <text>` waits for the text on the console, `send <text>` types it
# (\n is a newline). The kernel is booted with rdvirt.shell, so /init ends in
# a shell on the console. Typing goes through the goldfish-tty's receive
# interrupt and its DMA into RAM, which the boot alone never exercises.
# The awk lines are floating point in a static glibc built for the 68881/2
# instruction set: on an MC68040 its FATAN, FSIN, FETOX and FLOGN are
# unimplemented and are emulated by the kernel's M68040FPSP (on the part, and
# on UAE040; QEMU's m68040 executes them itself -- doc/oracles.md).
expect rd-virt: shell
expect # 
send echo sum $((6*7)) product $((123*457))\n
expect sum 42 product 56211
send uname -m -r\n
expect 7.2.9-rd040 m68k
send grep -c virtio /proc/interrupts; grep CPU: /proc/cpuinfo\n
expect 68040
send awk 'BEGIN{print "fp", 1/3+2, 7*0.125}'\n
expect fp 2.33333 0.875
send seq 1 200 | md5sum\n
expect 304f7b9574921ad21a2bc72f200483d7
send echo hello > /tmp.txt; cat /tmp.txt; wc -c < /tmp.txt\n
expect 6
send ls /sys/class/rtc\n
expect rtc0
send echo $(for i in 1 2 3; do echo loop$i; done | sort -r)\n
expect loop3 loop2 loop1
send echo done\n
expect done
send poweroff -f\n
